WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.849
We’ve all heard about the impeachment process
against Donald Trump.

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:16.880
However, how many really understand what this
means, what impeachment actually consists

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.960
of and the history of this process in the
United States?

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.410
I can already tell you that this process is
much, much more complex than a mere debate

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:27.939
about the President’s impeachment.

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:35.640
You see, the American Constitution defines
impeachment as a tool to be used only in extremely

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.700
exceptional cases.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.670
With it, the legislative power can practically
reverse the people’s will.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.790
A will expressed through the election of the
president.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:48.700
Therefore, under very serious circumstances,
an impeachment implies the overriding of the

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.070
democratic electoral regime.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:57.970
We’re talking about a process designed for
cases in which the president has committed

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:02.850
serious crimes, crimes that must be clearly
demonstrated after a thorough prior investigation

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.049
by the House of Representatives.

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:12.360
Mainly, these crimes include cases of treason,
bribery, and other serious unspecified crimes.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:18.920
However, even with this explanation, we still
need more information to be able to fully

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.090
understand how impeachment works.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:27.410
That’s why in this video we’re not only
going to talk about this curious process,

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:31.780
we’re also going to take a look at its history
and talk about past cases of impeachment in

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:36.300
the United States, the circumstances that
caused them and the outcomes.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.820
And yes, why not, we’ll also do a round
up on the impeachment that the current President

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:47.240
of the United States, Donald Trump, is facing.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:55.560
Listen up.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.780
(IMPEACHMENT, A KEY COUNTERWEIGHT)

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.190
Something good about American democracy is
that it was designed specifically to limit

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.350
the power of politicians.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.879
You see, the curious thing about the United
States is that both the Constitution and the

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:11.610
US Bill of Rights are limiting.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:16.230
This means that if you read the US Constitution,
you’ll soon realize that what it truly says

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.439
helps prevent the State – or the people
who control the state – from exercising

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:27.400
excessive power over the rest of the citizens.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.330
This strongly differentiates it from most
of the world's constitutions, which focus

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.489
on defining the rights and obligations of
the people.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:41.760
And this is fundamental, essential for understanding
the meaning of impeachment in the United States.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.930
Allow me to explain; the Founding Fathers
established the possibility of dismissing

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:51.049
the president and other senior officials from
the very beginning.

00:02:51.049 --> 00:02:55.180
They defined the process as a political instead
of a criminal trial.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:59.139
That way, the president could be removed from
his functions due to serious crimes without

00:02:59.139 --> 00:03:07.620
any judge ruling for or against him at any
moment during the process.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.329
In fact, the only judge present during the
entire impeachment process is the president

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:14.919
of the Supreme Court, who’s responsible
for overseeing the operation of the political

00:03:14.919 --> 00:03:16.639
trial once it passes to the Senate.

00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:22.250
But the “sentence” – in this case, dismissal
– is decided solely and exclusively by the

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:23.250
senators.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:27.090
By this point many of you may be thinking...

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:32.230
But, wait, if this is a political process,
then it could be used against any president

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:34.470
who the opposition simply doesn’t like,
right?

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.150
Well, the truth is, no.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:44.499
In practice, that isn’t possible in the
North American system.

00:03:44.499 --> 00:03:48.700
To prevent the process being abused, a series
of complicated steps were established to not

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.319
only guarantee the completion of a justified
impeachment process, but also to limit the

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:59.719
number of cases beginning.

00:03:59.719 --> 00:04:05.159
So much so that to this date, in the almost
250 years of United States history, not a

00:04:05.159 --> 00:04:07.970
single effective impeachment has yet occurred.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:09.230
Not even one.

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:14.151
Moreover, in that entire time only two processes
have been started, well, three if we take

00:04:14.151 --> 00:04:16.570
the current one against Donald Trump into
account.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:20.440
Three in the entire history of the US.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:24.200
Now, how exactly does this process work?

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.530
What are the steps that lead to dismissal?

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:34.560
Well, first there needs to be a reason, evidence
that the president was involved in one of

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.240
the crimes mentioned in the Constitution.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.650
With this, any member of the House of Representatives
can propose an impeachment or political trial

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:42.650
under oath.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:48.960
In Trump’s case it was the President of
the House of Representatives, Nancy Pelosi.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:55.780
Then, the committees responsible for investigating
the case and determining the facts attributable

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:57.780
to the impeachment are designated.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.590
Finally, these committees write one or several
articles that are voted for individually or

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.199
jointly by the House of Representatives.

00:05:04.199 --> 00:05:12.400
This step is equivalent to presenting charges
against the President.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.970
To understand this better, the House of Representatives’
mission in this case is to present charges.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:22.810
If it does, the impeachment or political judgment
truly begins and the entire process is transferred

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:30.740
to the Senate, which plays the main role in
this entire story.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:35.310
So in fact it’s actually there, in the Senate,
where the political trial takes place.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.599
In this way, the Senate acts as a court.

00:05:37.599 --> 00:05:41.849
But instead of judges, prosecutors and jurors,
the members of the House of Representatives

00:05:41.849 --> 00:05:46.400
act as prosecutors, and the Senators as jurors.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:55.129
So, if the senators vote in favor of dismissal
with a two-thirds majority, this is equivalent

00:05:55.129 --> 00:05:58.510
to a jury convicting the accused in a normal
trial.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:02.501
A successful impeachment means nothing other
than the automatic removal of the president

00:06:02.501 --> 00:06:09.990
and the transfer of his powers to the Vice
President.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.250
It seems complex, doesn’t it?

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:13.389
Actually, it is, but it’s also quite logical.

00:06:13.389 --> 00:06:17.540
Think about it for a moment, it has to be
a very stringent process so that a president

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:23.830
can’t be dismissed impulsively, like someone
grabbing a bargain on Black Friday.

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:30.009
But, now that we know how it works, the next
big question is: what can we learn from the

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:32.889
history of political trials in the United
States?

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:37.000
As we told you earlier, besides Trump, there
are only two more cases to examine: those

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.970
of Andrew Johnson and Bill Clinton.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:43.490
And... in the case of Richard Nixon, well
his presidency actually ended with a premature

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:48.500
resignation and, despite what is commonly
thought, the impeachment process never even

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:49.500
started.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.430
But do you want to know more about what happened
in those cases?

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:59.710
Let’s take a look!

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:05.229
(TWO IMPEACHMENTS, ONE RESIGNATION AND ZERO
REMOVALS)

00:07:05.229 --> 00:07:10.160
The history of political trials in the United
States is short, very short.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.909
Only two presidents have been submitted to
them and neither one was dismissed.

00:07:13.909 --> 00:07:16.599
Perhaps the least known case is that of Andrew
Johnson.

00:07:16.599 --> 00:07:19.759
And it’s not surprising, because we’re
talking about a man who was president between

00:07:19.759 --> 00:07:24.220
1865 and 1869…

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:32.060
But... what did this man do to end up being
judged by the American Senate?

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:36.849
Well... after President Lincoln’s assassination
in 1865, Johnson, who was his vice president,

00:07:36.849 --> 00:07:37.849
became president.

00:07:37.849 --> 00:07:42.080
After he took command, his policies moved
far... far away from Lincoln's.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:45.260
He was basically a cowboy.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:50.300
To give you an idea, Johnson was both a member
of the Democratic Party and a dedicated white

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.300
supremacist.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:52.300
That is, he was extremely racist.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:59.669
Someone who would make Trump look like a poster
boy for multiculturalism and diversity.

00:07:59.669 --> 00:08:06.090
Yes, Andrew Johnson strongly defended the
slavery of African-Americans and dedicated

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:10.849
himself to vetoing each and every one of the
bills that Congress had passed to recognize

00:08:10.849 --> 00:08:12.789
the rights of freed slaves.

00:08:12.789 --> 00:08:16.949
He also began pardoning generals of the Confederate
States of America, who were the ones who had

00:08:16.949 --> 00:08:21.770
rebelled against the United States by starting
the Civil War.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:29.040
Those states, the states that rebelled, considered
Lincoln a threat to white supremacy, and they

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.620
strongly opposed his plan to abolish slavery
throughout the country.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:34.770
This really didn’t sit well with them.

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:40.349
And you also have to remember that some of
these states had a slave population of 49%.

00:08:40.349 --> 00:08:48.520
But if his actions already seem serious, wait
till I tell you about the final straw.

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:52.900
Johnson had the brilliant idea of ​​personally
firing and appointing secretaries for his

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:58.180
government, bypassing Congress’s validation,
which, as you can imagine, isn’t legal.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:02.870
Well... you can get an idea of just how complicated
it is to dismiss a president, when despite

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:08.710
all these serious accusations, Johnson was
saved by just one vote in the Senate.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:12.390
A single vote that prevented them from reaching
the necessary two thirds majority.

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:15.520
He was barely saved, but saved all the same.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:24.600
In fact, this was by far the closest a US
president got to being dismissed.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:28.310
The next “attempt” came with Richard Nixon,
more than a century later, after the infamous

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:35.870
Watergate Scandal in 1972, which I’m sure
you’ve all heard about.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:39.480
To refresh your memory, the Watergate Case
was a scandal that involved the president

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.930
and several CIA agents who were spying on
the Democratic Party.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:47.580
They raided Democratic party headquarters
and tapped calls to gain an advantage in Nixon’s

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:50.770
reelection as president.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:56.820
Later, during the investigation, it was discovered
that this illegal eavesdropping had been controlled

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:58.480
directly by the White House.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:03.080
This news triggered calls for an impeachment
process against the president in 1972.

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:09.780
In fact, dear friends of VisualPolitik, the
thing got so serious that literally everyone

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.370
turned their backs on Nixon.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:16.280
And because he didn’t want the investigation
to proceed – in case dirtier issues came

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:20.410
up, because, let’s face it, Nixon was a
rather shady president – after seven months

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:24.710
of deliberations he resigned the day before
the House of Representatives were to vote

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:25.840
to start the process.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:26.840
One day before.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:36.480
So, the truth is that the impeachment mechanism
was never launched in Nixon’s case.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.440
But it was launched against Clinton.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:42.440
Oh Clinton, Clinton...

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:44.030
Bill Clinton really messed up!

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:45.030
And he messed up very deeply.

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:47.500
I’m sure many of you have heard the name
Monica Lewinsky.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.640
Am I wrong?

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.940
At the end of the 90s, the affair, the slip,
the... extramarital meetings between the then

00:10:55.940 --> 00:11:00.290
intern, Monica Lewinsky, and Bill Clinton
were common knowledge in Washington, without

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:03.190
his wife, Hillary, knowing anything about
it.

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:07.710
But don’t be mistaken.

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:12.010
The reason why an impeachment process was
started against Bill Clinton didn’t have

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:15.080
anything to do with the president being unfaithful
to his wife.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:16.080
Not at all.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:20.220
The real reason, or at least the reason for
which the process was justified, were Clinton's

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:25.010
lies, as well as his pressuring of others
around him to also lie to hide a sordid story

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:38.970
that took place behind the closed doors of
the Oval Office.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:48.860
And believe me when I tell you that in the
United States – at least until Trump arrived

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:53.320
– catching a president in a lie – and
forcing those around him to lie – wasn’t

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:58.990
viewed well, not at all.

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:02.690
So now that we’ve looked at past impeachments
and how they work ... What about Trump?

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:03.690
Well... now it's his turn.

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:07.150
The truth is that things don’t look very
good, because everything indicates that he’s

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:13.450
done something very serious.

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:16.440
So what exactly are the accusations against
Trump?

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:21.780
Can this process affect him in 2020?

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:25.580
Listen up.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:30.110
(TRUMP, BIDEN AND UKRAINE.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:32.980
A JUICY PLOT?)

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:35.380
Trump's case... started a long way back.

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:36.750
And far away as well.

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:37.980
More specifically, in Ukraine.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:38.980
But...

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:46.820
What on earth does Trump have to do with Ukraine
and why is Joe Biden in this section’s title?

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:53.700
Well, the truth is that this is none other
than the latest chapter in the – often absurdist

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:54.790
– Trump presidency.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:58.890
Basically, everything indicates that Trump
asked the new Ukrainian president, Volodímir

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:03.510
Zelenski, directly by phone, to investigate
Biden and his relations with the gas company

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:07.870
Burisma, in which Hunter Biden, the son of
Joe Biden, who was Barack Obama’s former

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:12.410
vice president and one of the future Democratic
candidates for the White House, was a board

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:13.490
member.

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:21.740
Shortly before this call, Trump froze almost
$400 million in military aid to Ukraine, apparently

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:22.740
as a pressure method.

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:25.910
This could be considered a bribe in exchange
for a favor.

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:28.140
A quid pro quo.

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:32.890
But it doesn’t end there.

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:37.850
Even the US Ambassador to the EU, Gordon Sondland,
acknowledged being pressured by Trump to force

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:42.370
the Ukrainian government to investigate a
supposed theory that Ukraine would have interfered

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:45.270
with the 2016 American presidential elections.

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:49.660
And no, this time it wasn’t to favor Trump,
which Russia is accused of having done, but

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:55.160
to favor Hillary.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:59.010
And you can imagine how Ukraine’s investigations
are handled, especially if they’re done

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:02.010
under pressure from Washington.

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:07.450
In other words, Trump seems to be using the
United States government’s means and resources

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:11.320
to pressure governments from foreign countries
that have a more than questionable rule of

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:14.860
law to dig up dirt against his opponents.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:15.860
Unbelievable!

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:23.820
Given the 
seriousness of these claims, Congress decided

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:28.450
to open an impeachment process to thoroughly
investigate this entire matter, and to figure

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:32.600
out if President Trump bribed the Ukrainians
to gain campaign data.

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:36.190
And it doesn't end there.

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:42.880
Apparently, Trump created a kind of “parallel
diplomacy” with Ukraine, which lay outside

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:44.160
the institutional channels.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:50.770
He used his personal lawyer Rudy Giuliani,
even forcing Gordon Sondland to answer to

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:51.980
him.

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:56.440
Now, is this enough to kick out the president?

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:00.640
We don’t know, but what we do know is that
information keeps being uncovered, further

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:02.920
complicating Trump's situation.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:04.160
Information like this.

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:08.490
(AUDIO: “New Documents Reveal Details of
Pompeo’s Role in Ukraine Affair

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:12.840
Emails and documents released by the State
Department support testimony showing Secretary

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:17.600
of State Mike Pompeo enabled the Ukrainian
pressure campaign at the center of the impeachment

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:18.810
proceedings”.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:19.810
The New York Times)

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:22.800
(AUDIO: “According to testimony in the impeachment
inquiry, Ukrainian diplomats in Washington

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:27.990
knew there was a problem with aid as early
as July 25, the day Mr. Trump spoke with the

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:31.000
Ukrainian president by phone and asked him
to investigate his rivals”.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:32.000
- The New York Times)

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:33.610
The truth is that, for the time being, the
Republican Party doesn’t seem to want to

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:37.050
turn its back on the president under the current
accusations.

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:42.150
Especially when the elections are so close...

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:43.590
Politicians!

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:48.010
All this could change however if what is known
in the United States as a “smoking gun”

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:52.540
is unleashed, that is, if information that
could bring down the president, and that is

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:57.690
100% proven, demonstrated and explosive, is
revealed.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:04.070
But until that happens, and if it happens,
it’s normal for the Republicans to support

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:06.230
Trump in order to hold on to the presidency.

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:10.510
Also remember that in the Senate, which is
where the impeachment is decided, Republicans

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:12.820
hold a majority.

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:19.320
As it stands, the Democrats’ only hope would
be to find flagrant evidence that Trump really

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:23.040
bribed the Ukrainian authorities to do all
that is claimed.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:24.850
Undoubtedly, this is quite the drama.

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:27.080
We’ll be watching to see how this mess develops.

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:28.650
But now it's your turn.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:33.220
After knowing how impeachment works, how past
cases happened and why they happened, do you

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:38.000
think Trump could be dismissed or will this
process end up as another failed case?

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:42.300
What do you think of the political trial process
as a counterweight to the presidents’ power?

